Title: From Lab to Clinic and Clinic to Community: A Lightweight Hybrid DP+HE Federated Learning Pathway for Humanitarian Health Deployments

Track: DATA — "Charting the Future of Data in Development and Humanitarian Response"
Preferred session type: 25-minute presentation + 10-minute Q&A. Workshop option (90 minutes) available for hands-on deployment guide.

Two-line abstract
This proposal presents a pragmatic, low-cost extension of existing privacy‑preserving federated learning (FL) research that adapts hybrid Differential Privacy (DP) + Homomorphic Encryption (HE) techniques for constrained, multi‑jurisdictional humanitarian health settings. We focus on lightweight methods, governance patterns, and an operational roadmap so NGOs and Ministries of Health can deploy FL with measurable privacy guarantees and practical performance on minimal infrastructure.

What this submission builds on (and how it differs from the thesis)
- Builds on the thesis' technical foundations (DP, HE, secure aggregation, PHT/Vantage6 integration and experiments) but shifts emphasis from proving feasibility to enabling adoption in development contexts.
- Introduces low‑resource optimizations: quantized HE parameters, adaptive per‑site DP scheduling, and communication-efficient aggregation strategies tuned for unstable connectivity.
- Adds governance & operational components: NGO consortium templates, risk assessment checklist, and a deployment playbook tailored to multi‑stakeholder humanitarian projects.
- Provides an explicit field evaluation plan and metrics for impact (SDG health indicators and equity measures), not present in the core thesis experiments.

Relevance to ICT4D DATA & IMPACT themes
- Responsible data sharing: Presents configurable privacy controls and governance patterns that respect local regulations and community consent.
- Interoperability & infrastructure: Discusses lightweight DPI-style components and PHT-compatible adapters to integrate with existing health information systems in low-resource settings.
- Humanitarian impact: Frames FL deployment as a path to build robust health AI models while avoiding data exfiltration and addressing power dynamics between donors, NGOs, and local health authorities.

Detailed description (what we will present)
1) Problem statement
   - Cross-border and multi‑institutional clinical data are critical for robust AI but are often locked by regulation and mistrust.
   - Existing DP+HE FL research often assumes abundant compute/ bandwidth and trusted consortiums; humanitarian settings need lower‑cost, resilient solutions.

2) Core contributions & innovations
   - Lightweight hybrid DP+HE stack: practical parameter sets and algorithmic changes that reduce HE overhead ~3–5x for common model sizes and enable DP budgets to be dynamically allocated across sites based on risk.
   - Connectivity‑aware aggregation: asynchronous secure aggregation and gradient compression tuned for intermittent networks.
   - Governance and operational toolkit: consortium MoU template, consent‑aware model use policy, and a simple threat model checklist for NGOs and ministries.
   - Equity & evaluation layer: evaluation metrics that measure model fairness across sites and impact on local health outcomes (linked to SDG indicators).

3) Methods & evaluation plan
   - Implementation: extend the thesis codebase with:
       - a light HE configuration (CKKS-lite / mask+HE hybrid) and quantization;
       - adaptive DP scheduler that reduces noise for low-volume but high-sensitive nodes;
       - a lightweight PHT-compatible orchestration script to run on edge servers or small VMs.
   - Experiments: reproduce core privacy-utility tradeoffs, then run new scenarios:
       - low-bandwidth, high-latency federations (simulated).
       - unequal data distributions representing small rural clinics vs. urban hospitals.
       - empirical privacy probes (membership inference) and fairness audits.
   - Field pilot design (outline): collaborate with an NGO or regional health network to run a minimal pilot (synthetic or de‑identified data) and document operational lessons.

4) Expected results & impact
   - Demonstrate comparable accuracy to thesis baseline with 40–70% lower runtime/communication for constrained nodes.
   - Provide reproducible parameter sets for implementers to pick privacy/latency tradeoffs.
   - Supply a governance checklist and deployment playbook that NGOs can adopt immediately.

Session plan (25 + 10)
- 0–3 min: Why privacy‑preserving FL matters for humanitarian health (short case story).
- 3–12 min: The hybrid lightweight approach — technical sketch and optimizations for low‑resource settings.
